The data are available over the globe at the spatial resolution of 0.1° and with the temporal extent from January 2007 to present.","identifier":"EO:CLMS:DAT:SWI_12_5KM_DAILY_V4","name":"Soil Water Index 2007-present (raster 12.5 km), global, daily - version 4"}

{"@context":"http://schema.org/","@type":"Dataset","url":"https://navigator.eumetsat.int/product/EO:CLMS:DAT:LWQ_REPROC_300M_10_DAILY_V1","copyrightYear":"2002","dateCreated":"Tue Jan 01 00:05:00 UTC 2002","datePublished":"Tue Jan 10 00:01:00 UTC 2012","isAccessibleForFree":"false","keywords":"Water,turbidity,GLOBE,environmental monitoring,10 days, 0:00:00 composite,aquatic environment,water monitoring,World,Global,SUSPENDED_SOLIDS,dam,inland water,phytoplankton,freshwater,Orthoimagery,EARTH_SCIENCE-OCEANS-OCEAN_OPTICS-WATER-LEAVING RADIANCE;EARTH_SCIENCE-TERRESTRIAL_HYDROSPHERE-WATER_QUALITY_WATER_CHEMISTRY-CHLOROPHYLL,Dekad,remote sensing,climate,monitoring,water,lagoon,seasonal variation,water reservoir,water quality,freshwater quality,NetCDF,TURBIDITY,algal bloom,lake,environmental data","schemaVersion":"3.5","spatialCoverage":{"@type":"Place","geo":{"@type":"GeoShape","polygon":"-179.9989  89.9989  179.9989  -89.9989"}},"temporalCoverage":"Tue Jan 01 00:05:00 UTC 2002 / Tue Jan 31 00:03:00 UTC 2012","thumbnailUrl":"https://sdi.eea.europa.eu/public/catalogue-graphic-overview/clms_global_lwq_300m_v1_10daily-reproc.png","encodingFormat":"NetCDF","description":"The Lake Water Quality products provide a semi-continuous observation record for a large number of medium and large-sized lakes, according to the Global Lakes and Wetlands Database (GLWD) or otherwise of specific environmental monitoring interest. 0-daily observations are available in near real time in the spatial resolution of 300m and and with the temporal extent from 2002 to 2012.

Monitoring water quality in lakes and reservoirs is key in maintaining safe water for drinking, bathing, fishing and agriculture and aquaculture activities. Long-term trends and short-term changes are indicators of environmental health and changes in the water catchment area. Directives such as the EU's Water Framework Directive or the US EPA Clean Water Act request information about the ecological status of all lakes larger than 50 ha. Satellite monitoring helps to systematically cover a large number of lakes and reservoirs, reducing needs for monitoring infrastructure (e.g. vessels) and efforts.

They consist of three water quality parameters: (i) the turbidity of a lake, that describes water clarity or whether sunlight can penetrate deeper parts of the lake. Turbidity often varies seasonally, both with the discharge of rivers and growth of phytoplankton; (ii) the trophic state index that is an indicator of the productivity of a lake in terms of phytoplankton, and indirectly, over longer time scales, reflects the eutrophication status of a water body and (iii) the lake surface reflectances that describe the apparent colour of the water body and are intended for scientific users interested in further development of algorithms. The visual reflectance bands can also be combined into true-colour images.","identifier":"EO:CLMS:DAT:LWQ_REPROC_300M_10_DAILY_V1","name":"Lake Water Quality 2002-2012 (raster 300 m), global, 10-daily - version 1"}

